//! What a call's **arguments** have to be.
//!
//! `returns.rs` answers "what type does this call produce", harvested from the
//! declared type of the temporary a `.pex` stores the result into. That says
//! nothing about arguments — and arguments are what decide whether the compiler
//! inserts a `cast` before a call. The real compiler showed that directly:
//!
//! ```text
//! Self.MoveTo(oProp, fProp, fProp, iProp)
//!   -> ::oProp_var ::fProp_var ::fProp_var bare, and iProp -> cast ::temp0 ::iProp_var
//! akA.MoveTo(oProp, afV, afV, afV)
//!   -> every argument bare
//! ```
//!
//! `MoveTo` wants `(ObjectReference, float, float, float, bool)`. `oProp` and
//! `fProp` already match those slots; `iProp` is an int where a float is
//! required, so it is cast. Args are converted **type-directed**, not
//! materialised blindly.
//!
//! # Where this table comes from
//!
//! The shipped `.psc` corpus. A `.psc` declares its own functions with real
//! parameter types, and that corpus includes the engine's own scripts —
//! `ObjectReference.psc`, `Actor.psc`, `Game.psc` and the rest — so the engine's
//! signatures are readable directly instead of guessed from documentation.
//!
//! `tools/params.py` reproduces the harvest: 27,593 declarations, 3,750
//! distinct function names, of which **3,674 have one observed signature** and
//! 76 have more than one. Only the unambiguous ones are kept. Ambiguity is real —
//! `ondeath` is `(Actor)` 367 times and `()` 6 times, because an event and a
//! function share the name on unrelated classes — so a name that means two
//! things is left out rather than resolved by majority.
//!
//! # What this does not claim
//!
//! - A method never declared in a shipped script is absent, and its arguments
//!   are left unconverted rather than guessed at.
//! - Method names are keyed without their class. The same name on two unrelated
//!   classes collapses to one entry when they agree and is dropped when they do
//!   not, so a signature here is a fact about the *name*.
//! - Parameter names are dropped. The types are kept because the cast decision
//!   depends on the type, and the defaults are kept because the CK **writes out
//!   a literal for every omitted trailing argument**:
//!
//!   ```text
//!   source:  BlockActivation()     ->  callmethod blockActivation self ::NoneVar true
//!   source:  Activate(akActionRef) ->  callmethod activate ::temp0 ::temp1 actronaut false
//!   source:  GetLinkedRef()        ->  callmethod getLinkedRef self ::temp0 None
//!   ```
//!
//!   Emitting the call short gives the loader a different argument count from
//!   the one the game shipped.

/// `(class, parent)`, lowercased, from every `Scriptname X extends Y`.
